   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the parks, recreation and historic preservation law and the vehicle and traffic law, regarding snowmobile registrations",
    "basePrintNo": "S4542",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S4542-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 50,
       "fullName": "John A. DeFrancisco",
       "imgName": "376_john_a._defrancisco.jpg",
       "incumbent": false,
       "memberId": 376,
       "sessionMemberId": 939,
       "sessionYear": 2017,
       "shortName": "DEFRANCISCO"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §27.15, Pks & Rec L; amd §2230, V & T L",
    "lawSection": "Parks, Recreation and Historic Preservation Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S4542\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\n\nAn act to amend the parks, recreation and historic preservation law\nand the vehicle and traffic law, regarding snowmobile registrations\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThis bill increases the penalties for operating an unregistered\nsnowmobile, failure to renew a registration, and for a violation\nregarding the improper display of snowmobile registration numbers.\nThis bill also increases the amount of state aid allocated to\nmunicipalities for snowmobile law enforcement purposes.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends subdivision 2 of Section 27.15 of the parks,\nrecreation and historic preservation law to increase the cap and\nlevels of state aid to municipalities for snowmobile law enforcement.\n\nSection 2 amends subdivision 2 of section 2230 of the vehicle and\ntraffic law to increase the penalty for operating an unregistered\nsnowmobile on the public trail system, lower the fine for failure to\nrenew a registration which was valid within sixty days, and lower the\nfine for improper display of the registration number.\n\nSection 3 establishes the effective date.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nThe entire NYS snowmobile trail system is built upon the revenue which\nis derived from snowmobile registrations. The entire registration fee,\nwith the exception of $5, goes into the trail fund. Over time, the fee\nfor a snowmobile registration has risen from $15 to $100; however, the\npenalty for operating without a valid registration has not been\nadjusted along with the fee increase.  The current penalty does not\nprovide for an adequate deterrent to riding without a registration and\nprovides little opportunity for the court to impose more serious\npenalties for habitual violators.\n\nThe amount of money available to local law enforcement has not been\nadjusted since the original Trail Fund was established in 1985. This\nbill increases both the amount available to law enforcement and the\npercentage of reimbursement in order to provide additional resources\nand ensure that sleds are properly registered.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2015-2016: A.10678 - Referred to Committee on Tourism, Parks, Arts and\nSports Development\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nTo Be Determined.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on January 1, 2018.\n",
